0..76 Comment "// A fold into the paths of a set respects its type\'s rules however its arms"
77..141 Comment "// compose those paths. The integers, presented by successor and"
142..218 Comment "// predecessor with the two cancellations as rules, have no cells: two paths"
219..294 Comment "// between the same two integers are one path. So addition\'s commutativity,"
295..369 Comment "// whose arms run the recursive call through a lemma that nothing reduces,"
370..442 Comment "// meets each rule once its two sides\' endpoints convert, and the kernel"
443..518 Comment "// re-derives it. The circle\'s words, presented by the two cancellations of"
519..596 Comment "// the loop and its inverse, form a set the same way, so a path at every word"
597..672 Comment "// whose arms disagree on the nose agrees by its endpoints. A fold into the"
673..746 Comment "// circle\'s loops, which are no set\'s paths, is still held to its arms as"
747..823 Comment "// written. A set is the HoTT book\'s Definition 3.1.1: any two paths between"
824..853 Comment "// two of its points are one."
854..856 Comment "//"
857..891 Comment "// - see: #427 — `plusCommAlong`"
893..951 Comment "// The circle and the presented integers with their steps."
